@ id: dopd02
@ order: 621
@ title: The mechanism — Δ_T token-level implicit reward, on-policy
@ subtitle: the teacher's log-ratio scores the student's own token choices
@ source: verl/verl/workers/actor/dp_actor.py::_compute_delta_opd_rm_scores

--- note
## The teacher's RL shift as a dense token-level reward
Direct-OPD needs two frozen teacher models: the **post-RL teacher** πT and the **pre-RL reference teacher**
πT_ref (the same model *before* its RL). Their log-prob gap on any token y given context x is the
**token-level implicit reward**:

    Δ_T(y | x) = log πT(y | x) − log πT_ref(y | x)

Read literally: **"how much more (or less) likely did the teacher make this token during its RL."** Δ_T > 0 on
tokens RL taught the teacher to prefer; Δ_T < 0 on tokens it learned to avoid. It is a *dense* signal — a value
at **every token position**, not a single end-of-sequence scalar — which is why it can carry the teacher's
credit assignment.

--- note
## On-policy: the student generates, the teacher scores
The crucial word is **on-policy**. The rollouts come from the **current student**, not the teacher:

- the student autoregressively generates a response (its own trajectory);
- at each position the two teachers are evaluated on the tokens the **student** actually considered (its
  top-k candidate ids), giving Δ_T on the student's own choices;
- the student is trained to move toward the tokens where Δ_T is positive.

So the student is never asked to reproduce the teacher's *sequences* or its *endpoint distribution*. It is asked
to **replicate the teacher's directional shift, evaluated on its own behaviour**. This is what lets a stronger
student exceed the teacher: the direction is applied from the student's better base, sharpened by the student's
own capacity, on the student's own trajectory distribution.

--- note
## Student-weighted, so it stays on-policy where it matters
The dense reward is not raw Δ_T — it is Δ_T **weighted by the student's own (detached) probability** over its
top-k candidates:

    weights   = softmax( student_logp.detach() )      # the student's own distribution, no gradient
    rm(y)     = weights(y) · Δ_T(y)

So candidates the student would actually emit dominate the reward, and candidates it already ignores contribute
little. The teacher's log-ratio and the weights are all **detached** — they form a *reward*, not a
differentiable target. The student's gradient enters only through the policy update that this reward drives
(dopd03). This is the exact function `_compute_delta_opd_rm_scores` in the cloned code; we reproduce its unit
vectors bit-for-bit in dopd06.

--- image
learning/assets/direct-opd/dopd_schematic.png
Same schematic as dopd01. The GREEN arrow is Direct-OPD: the student rolls out, the teacher log-ratio Δ_T
scores the student's tokens, and the student moves in that direction from its stronger base — replicate the
SHIFT, not the endpoint.

--- note
## Two reward modes in the code
The repo ships two forms of the same idea (`reward_mode`):
- **`delta_opd`** (dense top-k): the student-weighted Δ_T over the student's top-k candidate ids at each
  position (`log_prob_top_k=16` in the launch script). This is the default and the richest signal.
- **`delta_opd_sampled`** (single realised token): Δ_T on just the token the student actually sampled,
  `rm = (logπT(y_t) − logπT_ref(y_t)) · response_mask`. Cheaper, no top-k log-probs needed.
Both are token-level and detached, and both feed the same advantage estimator in dopd03.
